    {
      "question": "e-21671",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "1f35ed2c5e9be14a3257b4dc5c2907682e9c9159a071e17470f75f4f4a696fbe",
      "new_sha256": "1bf4b10ee88521c3da2a671a0c6c9483821fa56832d67feab05ca5696078c03b",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-21671",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\dfrac43,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac43 - 1 = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394y & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{\u2032}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{\u2032})\\cdot \u0394x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x-y+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}})-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\u0394y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}})-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = 1 + \\dfrac13 = \\dfrac43.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+5\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \u22480.67.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\!\\left(\\dfrac43\\right) \\approx 0.67.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14047192/example-21671.json",
          "file_sha256": "3937964d18181038d72169fed661a985407eed5b09296df2c952faef54978257"
        }
